s check `sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py` as it contains plotting logic and might be a good reference point for adding our new function.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n\n"}, {"content": "Since `sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py` did not yield specific plotting functionalities either, let's consider adding the `plot_calibration_curve` function to a more generic plotting module. A good candidate would be `sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py` since it often contains base classes and utilities for plotting.\n\nWe'll start by examining `sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py` again to see if we can identify a suitable place for our new function.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py</parameter>\n<parameter>search_terms>plot</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n\n"}, {"content": "Since `sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py` did not yield any specific plotting functionalities, let's explore `sklearn/metrics/_plot/__init__.py` to understand the initialization and imports of the plotting module. This might give us clues on where to add the new `plot_calibration_curve` function.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>sklearn/metrics/_plot/__init__.py</parameter>\n<parameter>search_terms>plot</parameter>\n\nuser\nEXECUTION RESULT of [search_code_snippets]:\nOBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"plot\" in sklearn/metrics/_plot/__init__.py...\n### Search Result:\n```\n14 | from ._det_curve import DetCurveDisplay\n15 | from ._precision_recall_curve import PrecisionRecallDisplay\n16 | from ._roc_curve import RocCurve